 brief={'objective':'Build a quarterly competitive monitoring mission.','audience':'B2B SaaS marketing team, 5 direct competitors.','constraints':'4 hrs/quarter; must drive decisions, not decks.'},
 reasoning_trace="""Objective: quarterly brief that changes ≥1 roadmap or budget decision. Baseline: ad-hoc competitor watching. Target: 1+ decision influenced/quarter. Kill condition: two quarters with zero decisions influenced — the brief is theater; kill the mission. Trust-ladder: briefs propose-only; any response to competitor moves (pricing page changes, counter-messaging) is separately approved — never reactive autopilot. Sequencing: the tracking set first (the asset — share of voice by intent segment, 1s-ahrefs-blog#6; pricing/packaging changes; review sentiment), then the quarterly review ritual, then the decision log. Systematic research beats anecdotes (wikipedia-marketing-clusters#20). Checkpoints: quarterly review (the mission is the checkpoint). Cost budget: $0; 4 hrs/quarter. No invented precision: baselines are measured, targets are commitments, unknowns are stated as unknowns.""",
 output="""Mission: "Quarterly brief that changes ≥1 decision." Baseline ad-hoc → target 1+ decision/quarter. Kill condition: two quarters, zero decisions — kill the mission, it is theater. Steps: (1) tracking set — share of voice by intent segment, pricing changes, review sentiment; (2) quarterly review ritual; (3) decision log. Briefs propose-only; competitive responses separately approved — never reactive. Checkpoints: the quarterly review itself. Cost budget: $0. Competitive intel that does not change a decision is a hobby — this mission has a kill condition for hobbies.""",
